> but I'm not at all certain I have it installed correctly,

Yes, that can get weird on Windows.  Unix-style operating
systems define specific directories at the system level
where certain things go.  Among other things, libraries
intended to be dynamically linked go in one place, and the
header files for them go in another.  Windows, however, was
originally designed under the assumption that libraries fall
into one of two categories:  ones that come with the
operating system, and ones that are part of an individual
application.  So there's no specific place to put libraries
intended for various arbitrary programs to be linked
against.  There are workarounds for this (e.g., shared
directories in Program Files), but it's fundamentally
different from the situation on Unix.

Also, PATH management on Windows is a great deal more
difficult than on Unix-type systems, because of fundamental
differences in the directory layout philosophy.  (The
Windows way of doing this actually has some advantages of
its own, but it undeniably makes some tricky issues for PATH
management.)  This applies not just to the regular PATH for
program executables but also to library paths.

So yes, depending on how you installed PCRE, it is possible
that the compiler doesn't know where to find it to link
against it.  This is somewhat easier for packages that use
automake/autoconf (even though *in general* automake and
autoconf are a royal pain on Windows and other non-POSIX
systems), because you can pass command-line arguments to the
configure script to tell it where you've put the libraries.
But I don't know how to do something similar when compiling
NetHack, which I think is older than automake and autoconf.
A real C programmer could probably edit the makefiles and
thus get it to work, but the whole idea of editing makefiles
scares me to death, as I've heard that they have significant
whitespace in them and may even make a distinction between
spaces and tabs (which, if it is actually true, is a feature
that would be right at home in INTERCAL).

Note too that a pre-compiled binary-only copy of the regex
library is only helpful if you're installing pre-compiled
things that use it.  If you're compiling, you need a copy of
the library that includes header files, which the linker
needs.  One option is to compile the regex library yourself.

